cli_result.result:
{"content":"Root cause found and fixed: `sqlparse/sql.py`'s `Comparison.left` property was returning `self.tokens[1]` instead of `self.tokens[0]`. Since token 0 is the actual left-hand operand and token 1 is the whitespace separating it from the operator, this off-by-one bug caused `.left` to return a whitespace token instead of the real left operand (matching the reported `AssertionError: assert ' ' == 'foo'`). This affected all 'comparison with X' checks (keywords, floats, parenthesis, strings, identifiers, etc.) since they all rely on Comparison.left.

Fix applied: changed `return self.tokens[1]` to `return self.tokens[0]` in the `left` property of the `Comparison` class.

Verified manually with multiple comparison forms (`foo = NULL`, `foo = 1.0`, `foo = (1)`, `foo = 'bar'`, `foo = bar`) — all now correctly report `foo` as `.left` and the appropriate right-hand token as `.right`.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1","name":"finish","arguments":{"summary":"Fixed regression in sqlparse/sql.py: Comparison.left property was indexing self.tokens[1] (whitespace) instead of self.tokens[0] (the actual left operand), causing comparisons with keywords, floats, parenthesis, strings, and identifiers to return incorrect .left values (e.g. ' ' instead of 'foo'). Changed to self.tokens[0] and verified with multiple comparison forms that .left and .right now return correct tokens."}}]}
